WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.760
Watch what happens when a pristine, standardized glass gear is forced into a complex custom-built metal engine.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.600
It shatters instantly.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.720
This friction is the exact reality of reactivative development.

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.880
Major version upgrades are notoriously fragile,

5
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.400
frequently resulting in cryptic build failures that bring development to a halt.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.720
Developers spend days battling missing dependencies,

7
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.720
untraceable iOS build errors, and broken configurations,

8
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.040
even when they follow the official react native upgrade helper to the letter.

9
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.600
The root cause of this frustration stems from a pervasive industry misconception

10
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.560
about what the upgrade helper actually provides.

11
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.120
Engineers frequently treat the tools output as a literal line-by-line patch

12
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.720
designed to be copied directly into their specific code base.

13
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.440
Copying that syntax blindly acts exactly like our mismatched glass gear.

14
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.880
It forces standardized code into a bespoke environment,

15
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.600
immediately destroying custom infrastructure.

16
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.680
The upgrade helper only displays the delta between two pristine,

17
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.080
completely empty project templates.

18
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.720
There is absolutely zero real-world application context inside its engine.

19
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.600
The file names that require updates are the same,

20
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.400
but a production application's files possess entirely different structural hierarchies,

21
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.280
custom configurations, and bespoke logic.

22
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.560
To survive an upgrade, engineers must stop attempting to map syntax line for line,

23
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.680
instead, the process requires translating the provided diff into semantic intent.

24
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.280
The difference between a seamless upgrade and three days of downtime

25
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.360
lies entirely in understanding what the upgrade helper does not show.

26
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.200
To understand those limitations, we have to look at the underlying architecture

27
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.280
that powers the upgrade helper UI,

28
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.520
an automated repository called rn-diff-perch.

29
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.200
By observing how this repository generates data as read-every release,

30
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.360
we establish a safe protocol.

31
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.480
The automation script executes its first step.

32
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.760
It triggers a standard react native init command,

33
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.280
generating a completely blank project pinned to the older version.

34
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.320
For the second step, the script runs the exact same initialization command,

35
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.720
but targets the brand-new react native release.

36
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.080
Finally, the system runs a direct comparison between these untouched templates

37
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.200
to create a patch file.

38
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.920
At no point in this automated pipeline,

39
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.480
does the system analyze custom deep links,

40
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.760
push notification handlers, or state management architectures?

41
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.040
Contrast this pristine, empty output with a real brownfield application,

42
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.840
which contains years of complex native overrides and custom third-party integrations.

43
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.520
Because the generated diff is purely template-based,

44
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.400
a specific line number highlighted in the upgrade helper

45
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.360
will almost never match the line number inside a real project.

46
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.440
This is an intentional boundary of the tool's capability, not a flaw.

47
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.280
The helper provides the raw ingredients for an upgrade,

48
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.560
not the final recipe.

49
00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.480
Since the diff exclusively shows what the template changed,

50
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.200
the developer is solely responsible for safely projecting those changes onto reality.

51
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.440
Because developers hold the responsibility for that projection,

52
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.120
they must triage the diff's files into distinct categories of risk

53
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.120
before writing a single line of code.

54
00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.000
The first column is category one.

55
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:18.880
Always apply.

56
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.600
These are purely mechanical files,

57
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.240
like Gradle Properties and the Package JSON,

58
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.680
that control the build environment.

59
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.720
They must be replicated exactly as shown.

60
00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.120
Failing to apply these mechanically carries severe consequences.

61
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.040
Overwriting these files directly with the upgrade helper template

62
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.720
will silently wipe out a project's custom activity flags,

63
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.160
specific permissions, and carefully tuned deployment targets.

64
00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.320
For category two,

65
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.920
developers open their local files side-by-side with the diff

66
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.400
and surgically merge the intent of the change.

67
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.200
The final group is category three, review and skip.

68
00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.480
These are tooling files where your existing setup completely supersedes the default template.

69
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.720
Standard test templates or generated linting rules should be ignored entirely

70
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.720
if they conflict with your established pipeline.

71
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.840
Files like the Metro config will require specific structural updates from the diff,

72
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.320
but you must absolutely preserve your existing custom plugins and path aliases.

73
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.880
To summarize the triage sprint,

74
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.440
mechanical files break builds if ignored.

75
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.240
Semantic files break features if overwritten.

76
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.440
Tooling files break experience if accepted.

77
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.320
Successful navigation of the upgrade helper

78
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.880
requires accurately categorizing every single file before attempting a change.

79
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.840
Treating a highly semantic native configuration file

80
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.920
like a simple mechanical version bump

81
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.160
is the primary cause of upgrade regressions.

82
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.040
We can transition from theory to execution

83
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.280
by applying this triage methodology directly to the React Native 0.75 to 0.76 upgrade.

84
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.040
We store it by focusing on a category two Android file,

85
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.040
the app level build.gradle.

86
00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.440
Looking at this chart comparing Android API distributions,

87
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.680
the 0.76 template diff shows a necessary bump to target SDK version 35

88
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.840
and minimum SDK version 24.

89
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.200
The semantic impact of these numbers is significant.

90
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.520
Moving to SDK 35 forcefully activates the Android 15 edge-to-edge UI,

91
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.800
which immediately breaks standard status bar background colors.

92
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.920
Bumping the minimum SDK to 24 permanently drops support for all Android 7.0 devices.

93
00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.760
You must verify your own user analytics before applying this change.

94
00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.000
Shifting focus to iOS, we address the most critical category two file in the project,

95
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.360
appdelegate.swift.

96
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.040
This diagram illustrates the semantic workflow perfectly.

97
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.800
We have to identify what the diff is doing operationally,

98
00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.800
rather than looking at what it says literally.

99
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.480
In 0.76, RCT app delegate becomes the new base class.

100
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.880
It completely takes over the initialization of RCT host,

101
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.160
manages surface mounting, and handles the application lifecycle.

102
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.120
The literal diff simply shows the deletion of manual window setup code.

103
00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.560
The semantic intent is that the base class now handles the window.

104
00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.480
Therefore, manual setup must be removed from your code base,

105
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.520
even if your existing syntax looks nothing like the template.

106
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.440
The rule for custom applications moving forward is specific.

107
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.120
If the app requires bespoke window logic or root view controllers,

108
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.320
engineers must now override the create root view controller method instead of writing it from scratch.

109
00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.680
While merging these new react native base classes,

110
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.280
the developer must strictly preserve their own custom deep link handling

111
00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.120
and push notification configurations.

112
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.640
Semantic translation ensures these massive architectural upgrades are absorbed safely

113
00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.280
without severing the app's custom infrastructure.

114
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:57.680
Enterprise environments and CI pipelines often require applying these complex diffs offline

115
00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.240
without relying on the web-based upgrade helper.

116
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:08.240
The RN-diff purge CLI capability solves this by generating local patch files for offline inspection,

117
00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.960
allowing for a standard three-way git application.

118
00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.520
Once all manual merges and automated patches are complete,

119
00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.040
a strict sanity check protocol is mandatory before compiling.

120
00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:25.520
Developers must execute a specific sequence to clear old artifacts, run a Gradle clean,

121
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.960
execute a fresh pod install, and start a new metrobundler with a completely reset cache.

122
00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.720
Attempting to build the application in Xcode or Android Studio before purging these caches

123
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.040
will result in false positive errors that completely mask a successful upgrade.

124
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.080
This clear, forward-moving current is the result of proper methodology.

125
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.880
By shifting away from literal patching and enforcing strict semantic translation,

126
00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.200
developers take a notoriously brittle process and turn it into a predictable

127
00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.800
engineered workflow.

